<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">River Shannon</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The River Shannon is the longest river in Ireland, flowing about 360 kilometres from County Cavan south to an estuary on the Atlantic below <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/limerick">Limerick</a>. It separated Connacht from the provinces to the east and was a highway for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Viking</a> fleets, which founded Limerick on its estuary. The Shannon hydroelectric scheme at Ardnacrusha, completed in 1929, was a landmark of the Irish Free State.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/river-shannon.jpg" alt="River Shannon" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Laurel Lodged · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:River_Shannon_from_Drumsna_bridge.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Shannon drains a basin of 16,900 square kilometres, about one fifth of the island of Ireland. By tradition it rises in the Shannon Pot, a small pool on the slopes of Cuilcagh Mountain in County Cavan, flows through or between 11 counties and enters the Atlantic through a tidal estuary 102.1 km long, at the head of which stands <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/limerick">Limerick</a>. Its fall is slight: it rises only 76 metres above sea level, and it is shallow in places. It divides the west of Ireland, principally Connacht, from the east and south, and there are fewer than thirty-five crossing points between Dowra in the north and Limerick. Many different lengths have been given for it, since an estuary has no definite end.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Ptolemy's Geography of the 2nd century CE already names the river, which had been an important waterway since antiquity. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Vikings</a> settled in the region in the 10th century and used the river to raid monasteries deep inland; in 937 the Limerick Vikings clashed with those of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dublin">Dublin</a> on Lough Ree and were defeated.</p><p>In the 17th century the river formed a strategic boundary. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/irish-confederate-wars">Irish Confederate Wars</a> the Irish retreated behind the Shannon in 1650 and held out for two more years against English Parliamentarian forces. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oliver-cromwell">Oliver Cromwell</a> reputedly said the remaining Irish landowners would go &quot;to Hell or Connacht&quot;, meaning west across the river. In the Williamite War the Jacobites also withdrew behind it after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-the-boyne">Battle of the Boyne</a> in 1690, and Athlone and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/limerick">Limerick</a>, which command bridges, saw bloody sieges.</p><p>Navigation was improved from 1755, when Thomas Omer began lateral canals and locks between Lough Derg and Lough Ree. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/limerick">Limerick</a> stretch was opened in 1799, and paddle steamers arrived in the 1820s; by 1831 they carried 14,600 passengers and 36,000 long tons of freight. A railway from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dublin">Dublin</a> to Limerick opened in 1849 and passenger numbers fell sharply. One of the first projects of the Irish Free State in the 1920s was the Shannon hydroelectric scheme at Ardnacrusha, which replaced the old canal from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/killaloe">Killaloe</a> to Limerick.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The Royal Canal and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-canal">Grand Canal</a> connect the Shannon to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dublin">Dublin</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/irish-sea">Irish Sea</a>, and the Shannon–Erne Waterway links it to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lough-erne">Lough Erne</a>. Commercial traffic fell from the 1950s, but leisure boating has since grown steadily. The Shannon Callows, lowland areas along the river, are a Special Area of Conservation. Irish mythology names the river after Sionann, a granddaughter of Manannán mac Lir, drowned when the waters of Connla's Well burst forth after she sought wisdom there.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “River Shannon” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The identical name in Ireland, borne by the Gangani (Ireland) on the <b>Shannon</b>, has prompted theories of migration across the Irish Sea in either direction.</blockquote><a href="/state/gangani-britain">Gangani (Britain)</a></li><li><blockquote>Ptolemy's Geography of about 150 places the Gangani on the west coast of Ireland, south of the Auteini and north of the Velabri, usually located in County Clare or around the mouth of the <b>Shannon</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/gangani-ireland">Gangani (Ireland)</a></li><li><blockquote>The Free State's first government under W. T. Cosgrave won the civil war in 1923, built a civil service, an unarmed police force, the Garda Siochana, and the <b>Shannon</b> hydroelectric scheme.</blockquote><a href="/state/irish-free-state">Irish Free State</a></li><li><blockquote>Vikings had raided the <b>Shannon</b> since the 9th century, and in 922 Tomrair mac Ailchi established a permanent fleet base at Limerick, Hlymrekr.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-limerick">Kingdom of Limerick</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1318 Muirchertach O'Brien defeated the Normans at Dysert O'Dea, preserving Gaelic rule west of the <b>Shannon</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-thomond">Kingdom of Thomond</a></li><li><blockquote>The Uí Maine claimed descent from Maine Mór, said to have won lands west of the <b>Shannon</b> in the 5th century.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-ui-maine">Kingdom of Ui Maine</a></li></ul></section>
